      \field{abstract}{We present version 1.0 of the cosmological simulation code \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}\$, designed for simulations of large-scale structure formation. \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}{\textbackslash}, 1.0\$ contains a P\${\textasciicircum}3\$M gravity solver, with the short-range part implemented using a novel (sub)tiling strategy, coupled with individual and adaptive particle time-stepping. A primary objective of \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}\$ is ease of use. To this end, it has built-in initial condition generation and can produce output in the form of snapshots, power spectra and direct visualisations. \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}\$ is the first massively parallel cosmological simulation code written in Python. Despite of this, excellent performance is obtained, even comparing favourably to other codes such as \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {GADGET}\}\$ at similar precision, in the case of low to moderate clustering. By means of power spectrum comparisons we find extraordinary good agreement between \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}{\textbackslash}, 1.0\$ and \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {GADGET}\}\$. At large and intermediate scales the codes agree to well below the per mille level, while the agreement at the smallest scales probed (\$k {\textbackslash}sim 13{\textbackslash}, h/\{{\textbackslash}rm Mpc\}\$) is of the order of \$1{\textbackslash}, {\textbackslash}\%\$. The \${\textbackslash}scriptstyle\{{\textbackslash}rm {CO}\}N\{{\textbackslash}rm {CEPT}\}\$ code is openly released and comes with a robust installation script as well as thorough documentation.}
