      \field{abstract}{We present the first simulation-based inference ({SBI}) of cosmological parameters from field-level analysis of galaxy clustering. Standard galaxy clustering analyses rely on analyzing summary statistics, such as the power spectrum, \$P\_{\textbackslash}ell\$, with analytic models based on perturbation theory. Consequently, they do not fully exploit the non-linear and non-Gaussian features of the galaxy distribution. To address these limitations, we use the \{{\textbackslash}sc {SimBIG}\} forward modelling framework to perform {SBI} using normalizing flows. We apply {SimBIG} to a subset of the {BOSS} {CMASS} galaxy sample using a convolutional neural network with stochastic weight averaging to perform massive data compression of the galaxy field. We infer constraints on \$Ω\_m = 0.267{\textasciicircum}\{+0.033\}\_\{-0.029\}\$ and \$σ\_8=0.762{\textasciicircum}\{+0.036\}\_\{-0.035\}\$. While our constraints on \$Ω\_m\$ are in-line with standard \$P\_{\textbackslash}ell\$ analyses, those on \$σ\_8\$ are \$2.65{\textbackslash}times\$ tighter. Our analysis also provides constraints on the Hubble constant \$H\_0=64.5 {\textbackslash}pm 3.8 {\textbackslash} \{{\textbackslash}rm km / s / Mpc\}\$ from galaxy clustering alone. This higher constraining power comes from additional non-Gaussian cosmological information, inaccessible with \$P\_{\textbackslash}ell\$. We demonstrate the robustness of our analysis by showcasing our ability to infer unbiased cosmological constraints from a series of test simulations that are constructed using different forward models than the one used in our training dataset. This work not only presents competitive cosmological constraints but also introduces novel methods for leveraging additional cosmological information in upcoming galaxy surveys like {DESI}, {PFS}, and Euclid.}
